UPDATE tarot_cards 
SET meaning_upright = 'GENERAL ARCHETYPE: The Four of Swords represents the absolute archetype of "Systemic Maintenance"—the essential, high-restorative phase where the sovereign consciousness, having endured the surgical de-fragmentation of the Three, enters a state of intentional, deep-cycle hibernation. Moving beyond the piercing intensity of the Three, the Four signifies the stage of "The Sovereign Sanctuary," where you are not merely "pausing," but actively reallocating your processing resources toward core stability, healing, and structural integrity. Visually depicted as a figure resting on a tomb or stone bed with swords hanging overhead, this archetype embodies the concept of "Sovereign Restoration"—the realization that true strength is not found in perpetual, high-speed execution, but in the ability to recognize when your system requires a full, offline cycle of recalibration. Within the complex architecture of your psyche, this is the stage of "Resource Recovery," where you are running low-power background tasks to repair the damage caused by the previous high-impact cycles. When the Four of Swords appears, your system is registering a "Mandatory Maintenance" status; your cognitive energy is depleted, and you are being invited to stop attempting to "push through" and instead allow yourself a complete, protected period of silence. This card commands you to perform a brutal audit of your sustainability. You are being tasked with the essential work of "Intentional Stillness": withdrawing from the manifest field, silencing the input of the collective, and allowing your internal data to re-order itself in the absence of external pressure. To inhabit the Four of Swords is to master the art of "Strategic Withdrawal," recognizing that by choosing to step back and guard your internal sanctuary, you are not failing the campaign—you are proactively ensuring that you possess the necessary power to survive the next, inevitable surge of activity.',
meaning_reversed = 'GENERAL ARCHETYPE: When the Four of Swords appears in the reversed position, it acts as a high-intensity signal of "Systemic Over-stimulation," "Restoration-Collapse," or the catastrophic refusal to acknowledge that your internal fuel reserves are at critical levels—a state where you are obsessively attempting to maintain "active status" while your system is screaming for an immediate, full-cycle reboot. The reversal suggests that you are currently running a high-priority, high-cost maintenance script that equates "ceaseless productivity" with "true effectiveness," even as your actual, internal operating system is experiencing a total, non-recoverable performance drain. You are essentially "running the engine until it seizes," running a protocol that prioritizes the ego’s desire to "look useful" over the sober, unglamorous requirement to actually rest. This creates a massive, systemic misalignment: you are intellectually and behaviorally attempting to perform the role of the "tireless machine," while internally, you are being hollowed out by a profound, gnawing sense of burnout, leading to a state of chronic, high-latency irritability, the experience of "faking your way through the day," and a persistent, gnawing awareness that your current "output" is a low-quality, high-cost artifact of your own, stubborn refusal to shut down. From an analytical perspective, this is a clear sign that you are leaking your life force into a "feedback loop of depletion"; you are obsessively trying to force the system to perform without the necessary rest, refusing to accept that the only thing keeping you from the necessary, final, and often forced, reboot is your own, deep-seated fear that if you were to step back, you would have to acknowledge that the work you are "protecting" would likely function better without your constant, panicked interference. The reversed Four of Swords exposes the hidden, egoic fear that drives this behavior: the paralyzing terror of "being idle," and the corresponding fear that if you were to finally take the time to restore yourself, you would be forced to face the reality of your own, present-day, depleted self. This reversal commands an immediate, radical intervention: you must stop the output of these frantic, high-burnout scripts, perform a brutal audit of your energy levels, and confront the reality of why you were so hesitant to prioritize your sustainability over your output. It is a demand to stop the self-deception that keeps you tethered to a state of chronic, high-maintenance exhaustion, and to initiate the move toward a genuine, humble, and often terrifying reclamation of your own, individual authority. You are being tasked with the uncomfortable, yet essential, work of radical rest—accepting that the "stuckness" you feel is the direct result of having prioritized the comfort of "the machine" over the vulnerability of "the human," and realizing that until you are willing to step back, surrender to the silence, and allow your baseline to reset, you will continue to be starved by the very, phantom momentum you have worked so hard to simulate.'
WHERE id = 32;
